    "source": "The Power Of Your Network",
    "page": 2,
    "text": "Accelerator Program\nThe Power Of Your Network\nPage 2\nThe Power Of Your Network\nPathways & Long-Term Planning\nBuild The Next Move Before The Moment Arrives\nA career path is a strategic guide for the next several years. Success is rarely built overnight. The goal is\nnot reckless speed, but a steady ascent that keeps the artist moving with direction, stamina, and control.\nEvery major decision should answer two questions: what does this do for the career now, and what does\nit make possible next? A festival slot, release, showcase, collaboration, or press moment should never\nbe treated as an isolated event. It should fit into a larger sequence.\nPlans can change. A show may create unexpected momentum. A release may travel faster than\nexpected. A connection backstage may shift the next step. Still, the artist should never leave the stage,\nfinish a rollout, or complete a campaign without understanding the move that follows.\nThree-Year Direction\nUse a three-year view to measure movement, identify patterns, and decide whether the current plan\nneeds to continue or change.\nStrategic Timing\nNot every major opportunity should be taken immediately. Sometimes the better decision is to wait for the\nstronger slot, better release moment, or better audience context.\nNext Move Thinking\nEvery important action should create a logical next step. Momentum is easier to use when the next move\nis already mapped.\nFlexible Control\nA plan is not a cage. It is a control system that helps the artist adapt without losing direction.\nFocus matters. As Jimmy Iovine has explained, a racehorse wears blinders because looking left\nor right can cost a step. When pursuing something serious, outside noise cannot dictate the path."
