    "text": "Accelerator Program\nThe Power Of Your Network\nPage 7\nThe Power Of Your Network\nBuild Your Professional Contact List\nThe Roles Every Self-Managing Artist Should Understand\nA self-managing artist should gradually build a contact list across the professional classes that shape\nthe music industry. These contacts may not all become part of the artist team, but understanding who\nthey are and what they do creates leverage.\nSound Supervisors & Editors\nThese professionals help prepare, edit, place, and manage music for sync opportunities in film, television,\nadvertising, games, and other media. They may work with music supervisors, producers, composers, editors,\nand rights holders to make music usable for visual media.\nEntertainment Lawyers\nA music lawyer helps protect contracts, rights, copyright interests, licensing deals, publishing agreements,\nmanagement terms, and negotiation points. Artists should be careful entering serious agreements without\nlegal guidance.\nManagers\nManagers guide career development, strategy, business negotiations, branding, networking, and opportunity\nmanagement. This program supports self-management skills, but a strong manager can create major\nlong-term impact.\nImportant principle: if an artist is signed or independent, these professionals should serve the artist\ncareer goals and vision. They are not above the artist. They are professional support systems around\nthe artist."
