    "text": "The Business Behind The Artist\nThe Essentiality Of Contracts\nContracts are not paperwork for paperwork sake. They protect\nownership, clarify expectations, define payment, and make sure the\nwork does not become vulnerable when opportunity arrives.\nA serious artist treats contracts as part of the creative system. Songs, masters, publishing, visuals,\ncollaborations, production, management, licensing, and merchandising all carry value. If those\nrights are not documented clearly, the value can become exposed, disputed, or taken by someone\nbetter prepared.\nA contract is a written agreement built around mutual promises. It establishes who owns what,\nwho delivers what, how long the relationship lasts, how compensation works, and what happens if\nthe project changes. It is not meant to trap the artist. It is meant to protect the artist, the team, and\nthe work.\nContracts can evolve with the business, but they should never be ignored. The music industry\nmoves quickly. When a song, show, or opportunity suddenly becomes important, the agreement\nbehind it becomes the shield.\nCore Principle\nYour music, your time, your image, and your rights all have value. Contracts make that value\nvisible, protected, and enforceable.\nWorking Standard\nBefore serious work begins, define ownership, payment, credit, delivery expectations,\nresponsibilities, and duration in writing.\nArtist Mindset\nDo not treat contracts as fear. Treat them as structure. Structure protects creative freedom.\nAccelerator Program\nThe Business Behind The Artist\nPage 2"
