    "page": 3,
    "text": "The Business Behind The Artist\nContracts Artists Should Know\nThe following contract categories are not here to overwhelm you. They\nare here to show where music business rights usually live, and where\nprotection should begin.\nRecording Agreement\nDefines recording obligations, rights, delivery,\ncompensation, ownership, and release\nresponsibilities.\nDistribution Agreement\nCovers how music is delivered to platforms, how\nrevenue is collected, and what the distributor\ncontrols.\nPublishing Agreement\nDeals with compositions, songwriter income,\nlicensing, administration, and publishing rights.\nSync Licensing Agreement\nAllows music to be used in film, television,\nadvertising, trailers, games, and other media.\nMechanical License\nCovers reproduction and distribution of\ncompositions in formats such as downloads,\nstreams, and physical products.\nPRO Agreement\nConnects the writer or publisher to a performance\nrights organization for performance royalty\ncollection.\nProducer Agreement\nDefines producer fees, points, credits, ownership,\ndelivery obligations, and backend participation.\nManagement Agreement\nDefines the artist-manager relationship,\ncommission, term, services, authority, and\ntermination rights.\nMerchandising Agreement\nCovers rights, revenue, approvals, manufacturing,\nand branding connected to merchandise.\nWork For Hire Agreement\nClarifies that commissioned work is being created\nfor another party under specific ownership terms.\nAgent Agreement\nDefines booking authority, commission, territory,\nterm, and live performance responsibilities.\nLegal Counsel Agreement\nDefines the relationship between the artist and the\nlawyer handling contracts or advisory work.\nAccelerator Program\nThe Business Behind The Artist\nPage 3"
